<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Jaswan State</h1><p class="st-meta">1170 CE – 1849 CE (approx.) · capital Rajpura</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Katoch Rajput hill state in the Jaswan Dun, an offshoot of Kangra whose Raja lost his lands after joining the revolt of 1848.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Jaswan was founded as a branch of the Katoch dynasty of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kangra-state">Kangra State</a>; tradition places the split around 1170. Its capital was Rajpura in the Jaswan Dun, the valley of the Sohan near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/una-himachal-pradesh">Una</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A small agricultural state, Jaswan was often dominated by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kangra-state">Kangra</a> and later by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> annexed it in 1815, leaving the Raja a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagir">jagir</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The British confirmed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagir">jagir</a> in 1846, but Raja Umed Singh joined the rising of the hill chiefs during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-sikh-war">Second Anglo-Sikh War</a> in 1848, and his lands were confiscated in 1849. The area is now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/una-himachal-pradesh">Una</a> district, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himachal-pradesh">Himachal Pradesh</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/jaswan-state.jpg" alt="Jaswan State" width="320" height="430"><figcaption>Image: Surveyed by Alexander Cunningham, drawn by Kalleemoodden · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Detail_of_the_main,_continuous_tract_of_territory_of_Jaswan_(Jaswal)_from_a_map_of_the_various_Hill_States_of_the_Punjab_Hills_region,_copied_in_1852.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1170 CE – 1849 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Rajpura</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Umed Singh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kangri</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>India</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Jaswan</dd></dl></aside></div>

